[Music]  I'm excited to have Priyanka Vergara a  customer engineer at Google cloud here  with me to show us how to set up service  level monitoring and stackdriver hi  franca hi Stephanie thank you for having  me thank you  Google has published our practices for  managing reliable services at scale in  site reliability engineering books and  this sre workbook one of the main  principles of sre is using service level  objectives can you show us how  developers can do the same yeah let's  dive right in and let me show it through  a demo okay great perfect so I have this  website here that is built to order some  stuff and so I'm gonna put something in  the cart and it's it's an e-commerce  website it lets you order things and  then it places an order so I have my  credit card information in there and we  just placed an order now behind the  scenes there's a lot going on in this  particular application so if we look at  what's going on here this is the service  architecture of the application and it  is talking it has a front-end and has  various different services talking to  the front-end  check out the catalog the product  catalog the card service this is a lot  that's going on here and if you see  further they're pretty much all written  in very different languages as well so  there's a lot of complexity behind the  scenes that's that's going on and this  is all done through micro services as  you as you can see so it's it's  important to understand that a simple  website is set up with with different  lengths can be set up with different  services in the backend and have  different languages that it can be  written in so complexities right and  micro servers architectures mean the app  itself is broken into many small pieces  and doesn't that make it hard to  understand how all the pieces fit  together and where to start  investigating when there is a problem  it's very it becomes very hard for the  operations team especially in micro  services architecture to figure out  what's going on which services is up  down talking to each other so it there's  a good tool in stackdriver monitoring  called  topology where you can actually look at  the topology of your different micro  services talking to each other so I have  a  been here and when you open the service  topology I can look at a specific  service that I'm interested in and then  see which other services it is talking  to so it gives me a view I can click on  one and it gives me a view of what's  going on and which services it is  talking to you which is pretty helpful  when you're working in an environment  that has so many different services  micro services that it's talking to well  and this is great because with micro  service based apps you can clearly see  the inter-service dependencies and use  that to double-check new code rollouts  but let's say you're setting up alerts  op staff can burn out a lot of the times  from getting notified of all the  potential causes of a problem making it  hard to align S aureus with customer  interests how can we identify which  behaviors really actually matter for  that service during service degradation  yeah so there is also another tool  within stat driver monitoring for  services where you can actually set up  SL o--'s and define which define your s  allows for the services so operations  does not actually get affected by just  looking at all the different alerts that  that can come by it's actually a  practice within our sre teams where we  make sure that our there are a sorry  staff is only monitoring alerts that are  related to customer issues and and  front-end customer related issues and  not every single issue that might be it  might be a cause to those issues so  let's let's see that in a in the demo as  well yeah so if we click on services  here in the staff driver monitoring  let's look at front-end and when we go  do that you're able to see the service  level optimization so let me click on s  loz and in SLO as you obviously see  nothing here so we're going to set one  up and say we want to set up for  availability and then there is we can do  a rolling or calendar I want to say in  the month if we reach 99% or no  availability then I want to get alerted  that is that's a good way of saying that  you're not gonna be alerted for  everything but you still want to be  alerted for when we go  less than 99% and then you can also set  up a latency alert so in this case if we  say latency and I want to set up  differently for different services for  the front-end you could say my pager  should load in less than thousand  milliseconds otherwise I want to be  notified and in this case also let's say  it's 90% our goal and then we submit  that so these are the two alerts that we  just set up for latency and availability  and they are not just thresholds because  we are saying this percentiles so it's  not going to be charged at E and noisy  it's going to be if this happens then we  really want to get notified because the  service is not working fine  so that's that's a good way of  identifying making sure that you're not  burning out your your operations to  excellent how do you follow these best  practices given the resources that you  have yeah so you also have metrics and  Diagnostics in there so if you click on  metrics you're able to see the request  count and the response latency is which  which will guide you through the request  and response and if you click on  Diagnostics that actually takes you to  the trace so now if I want to drill  deeper into the 95th percentile which is  probably the one I want to look at  because it's not performing very well  then I can click on that and see a  specific request why it was slow for for  what reasons and which requests were  actually so so in this case we can see  that the checkout service was a little  bit slow in send and receive so we may  want to do deeper into that and find out  what's going on there so the Diagnostics  tab and the metrics tab are really good  friends for troubleshooting great and at  some point you probably need to dig a  little deeper into the services signals  let's say maybe you received an SLO  alert and there's no obvious upstream  cause or you have a customer complaint  outside of an SLO alert that you need to  investigate or maybe you need to see how  the new rollout of your code version is  going how do you get a coherent display  of all the signals for a specific  service yeah so I think the best the  best thing to do at that point is you  you got an alert through SLO you want to  look at metrics and Diagnostics to kind  of figure out what's going on because  metrics and diagnostic  going to be helpful to understand which  specific services not performing well as  you can see in Diagnostics when we drill  deeper into a specific service we were  able to find out that checkout is the  culprit and so we can drill deeper into  that so those metrics and Diagnostics  would be your great friends in order to  find out what's actually going on so  stackdriver service monitoring gives you  a whole new way to view your application  architecture reason about its customer  facing behaviors and get to the root of  any problem that arises so thank you so  much for younger for showing us how  Google is transforming the opps  experience of cloud native and micro  service deployments thank you for having  me it was fun  [Music]  you

